#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "581d60ae-7926-5d60-a348-3c7fe045db06" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimensions of a Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "0f6dd7a4-d75c-587f-9c0e-511c634e52e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-17948" :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf y: \\mathbb R^3\\to \\mathbb R^2$ and $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^2\\to \\mathbb R^2,$ defined by\n![](images/cd/cd2fdbccada4cc5278411db56d86dc72e44aa183fbe9a0c564c4eee587072fef.svg)\n\nDetermine the dimensions of the matrix representing the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}= \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203869 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e07aa16a-05a4-5cf7-8eba-bfe287efdf88" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Composition of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a299b04b-ea8a-5e69-9c50-f1e25a1e2ef9" :question/math-academy-id "e-17949" :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf y: \\mathbb R^3\\to \\mathbb R^2$ and $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^2\\to \\mathbb R^2,$ defined by\n![](images/82/823c7500670ec7deeb16b8f656904010f2a579d40f8967ee5f9948f93a2d0c3d.svg)\nWhat is \n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))$ evaluated at \n![](images/31/311debaad69f328c5e9acc9ec0b218eded626be5a9e93f5b07837dc4c296fdaa.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/46/46e607310554414649de798f3b01d1965364d6d652e2ab308d4e5c2647d93ca2.svg) we get\n![](images/95/950fed4e51766a66f71026c05093c38764bc1f0bc9c4c5136015a4187c2c1154.svg)\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -{x}_{3} & -{x}_{2} \\\\ 1 & {x}_{3} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/54/547759836320b9850df4a70d8ba19a05741412cc0935dcc43caf8224da07fdce.svg) we get\n![](images/94/94bf8ffbcce681efde92719b220545ac89cdee324cd4dedf391b62e916a3ee8e.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12de0411-fa8a-5be0-9d80-3e174cf92707" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Composition of Three Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "1fb3c5e6-ead1-52be-aa8c-3543e69bac24" :question/math-academy-id "e-17950" :question/problem "For $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y, \\mathbf z\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)))$ evaluated at ![](images/76/7625625edbb65c637775812baec802a512b580b45e94c896764377cd23a0f946.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{z} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{C}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z} = \\mathbf{C}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{z}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n![](images/aa/aa560155df00efd7959768d56e38ef7fea999b06d2bfe5515b1af01f2184aaf4.svg)\nEvaluating at ![](images/9e/9ee03503efd8d3da6a4aa7e012eef1d0e390d014aa6ceae1b0f18acd82b5c08f.svg) we get\n![](images/73/73522ffceb4c0e0f4d3cad330414660ad822609c67a0a0024cdab2cb62c13778.svg)\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203873 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc95d2b5-9318-58b0-aa61-963954bf4594" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Functions Defined Using Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "2d9c4fa9-4d6a-5b0f-905d-0795fb26e9bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-18032" :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{y},$ where $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^3, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ and the matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are given by\n![](images/93/93f1a584378171c20649f8c1505162f580fad7074bbb73a571b2eff1ef53b57e.svg)\nWhat is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_0 = [1, \\, 0, \\, -2]^T?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{B}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} = \\mathbf{B}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 20 & -10 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "581d60ae-7926-5d60-a348-3c7fe045db06" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimensions of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829eaf69-efe6-543a-8e00-aaeeb5a7bff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "829eaf69-efe6-543a-8e00-aaeeb5a7bff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531148} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e8af08-6f3d-542f-9c27-ac0092d1e952" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99131c34-2864-51f3-a766-2f1711213fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99131c34-2864-51f3-a766-2f1711213fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531193} :answer-field/id #uuid "273ae1bc-6569-51f3-9b52-35e60079ae43" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1401d1ad-903b-53db-9c28-7363827d3400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1401d1ad-903b-53db-9c28-7363827d3400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530899} :answer-field/id #uuid "37608c1b-6b71-5eee-be39-b1d5820e3583" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62305239-9216-526d-90d5-03acac93db1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62305239-9216-526d-90d5-03acac93db1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531078} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b10feee-49bf-5f48-a752-82ca217efd7c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a551f62f-d8ff-5c79-9764-9e1a62f3bcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a551f62f-d8ff-5c79-9764-9e1a62f3bcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531221} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f00946b-80d3-591e-9f55-4d7a50d7a967" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247b61cb-8eb4-5114-8792-a2ebc10cf697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247b61cb-8eb4-5114-8792-a2ebc10cf697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce46bf49-6669-5133-a050-5509d649b2d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43f51336-4d87-5e3a-8c17-d2fcf2b0a3a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-268179" :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc95d2b5-9318-58b0-aa61-963954bf4594" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Functions Defined Using Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ddbd4f-c089-554f-8752-bca2aefecb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ddbd4f-c089-554f-8752-bca2aefecb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530949} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a64158-82fe-5faf-9558-e90e1bf35a07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f93605a-da0e-549c-b764-c0d84e5801bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f93605a-da0e-549c-b764-c0d84e5801bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531174} :answer-field/id #uuid "f49419a6-3cbc-587e-a23a-1593f5e536c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0b711b8-90eb-5c9a-bf0b-e912d2cf977d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0b711b8-90eb-5c9a-bf0b-e912d2cf977d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531323} :answer-field/id #uuid "f65aadad-0294-5698-8b70-3803c988a558" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab09796d-e835-5a4b-80f8-cb91d15672b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab09796d-e835-5a4b-80f8-cb91d15672b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531233} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d939315-b093-5b09-a0de-ed43d5556269" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27537bcf-01d8-5279-b7c5-d8bcb39361eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27537bcf-01d8-5279-b7c5-d8bcb39361eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530955} :answer-field/id #uuid "3edb74aa-1ee4-5cd8-90e7-6382208b7fa9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a0fdef7-e71b-5b7f-826f-b2eea8af1546" :question/math-academy-id "q-269242" :question/problem "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{3},y\\in {R}^{2},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 8 & 16 & 24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "581d60ae-7926-5d60-a348-3c7fe045db06" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimensions of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a342a32-6392-5dbb-a213-057659b94404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921