\ {y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 4$ matrix is a $2\\times 4$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "581d60ae-7926-5d60-a348-3c7fe045db06" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimensions of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd128f52-6c8f-5f8a-978e-423aa8c74c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd128f52-6c8f-5f8a-978e-423aa8c74c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531291} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a17a647-d699-507a-84b6-ca789a2ab305" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531405} {:answe